<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Candide</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1759">1759</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Candide, ou l'Optimisme</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Candide is a satirical novella by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a>, published in 1759, in which a naive young man travels the world and meets war, disaster and cruelty while his tutor Pangloss insists that all is for the best. A mockery of philosophical optimism, it is one of the most famous works of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>. Its episode of a mutilated enslaved man near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> is an early literary attack on colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/candide.jpg" alt="Candide" width="320" height="581" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Voltaire · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Candide1759.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
